# The report on the Pythagoras classroom

Written by Alice, the observer, at the user's request. It says what the
small experiment was, records how it went, and ends with what I conclude
from it. The longer record is in the files beside this one.

## The experiment in one paragraph

The user found the numbers of the main story hard to feel, and named the
Pythagorean theorem as the opposite: something plain that one can see is
true. So four agent sessions were made students and asked to prove
`a² + b² = c²` for a right triangle, each in a different way and temper,
in acts, with the user stepping in after each act. Two acts ran. They
produced about 1,850 lines. The user stopped it with "it became so
complicated" and asked for the proof itself, which is six lines.

## The proof

A square of side a + b holds four copies of the triangle in its corners,
and a tilted square of side c in the middle.

```
 +---a---+------b------+
 |      / \            |
 b    /     \          a
 |  /         \        |
 +      c × c   \      +
 | \              \  / |
 a   \            /    b
 |     \        /      |
 +---b---+------a------+

(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²        the square, as one square
         = 4 · (ab / 2) + c²    the same square, as its five pieces
         = 2ab + c²
so
a² + b² = c²
```

## Who took part

| Name | Part | What they did |
|---|---|---|
| Alice | observer and teacher | wrote the story, the briefs and the closings of each act |
| Bob | analytic, grumpy | the proof above, with coordinates to show that the middle is a square |
| Dave | geometric, cheerful | the proof above, in 25 steps, opening with two pictures |
| Carol | topological, quiet | showed that no proof from topology exists, then proved it by similar triangles |
| Percy | the doubter | proved nothing. Merged everyone's assumptions into one list of 37 |
| Omega | outside reader | took no part. Wrote a postmortem afterwards |
| Euclides | witness | took no part. Read every step of the three proofs afterwards and testified that they hold |

All seven are sessions of one model. The tempers are one sentence each in a
brief.

## How it went

| When | What |
|---|---|
| 03:15 | The branch `pythagorean` and the discussion open. Act 1: state the theorem, list what is taken for granted, prove nothing |
| 03:17 to 03:18 | Four parts written, 44 assumptions in all |
| after Act 1 | The user: "a lot of stuff is already artificial and pre-cooked… I don't see the difference in proofs." I agree, sketch both proofs in chat, and recommend dropping the staging |
| then | The user: "let's continue with Act 2." I run it as designed, in a stricter form |
| 03:27 to 03:30 | Three proofs of 21, 22 and 25 steps, and the common list of 37 facts |
| after Act 2 | The user: "What the heck is going on?… What I want to see as a proof is the hypotenuse square equals to the sides, square summed." I show the six lines |
| 03:33 to 03:40 | Three readings of what went wrong: mine, Percy's, Omega's |

## The protocol: what the task was, and where it came from

### Was there a problem to solve?

No. The user never stated one, and I never asked for one.

The user gave a dissatisfaction and a scene.

- **The dissatisfaction:** the health figure is only numbers, and "I
  don't have a feeling of it… it's not like a proof of a Pythagorean
  triangle".
- **The scene:** a story, the workers as students, three ways of proving,
  a personality each, a live session with acts, and a stop after each act.

The user called it "an experiment" and put the rest as questions: "Can we
do some basic consistent domain, and can we agree on things?" A problem
has something unknown in it, and a way to tell when it has been found.
The request had neither, and I treated it as if it had both.

### What task the students got

"Prove that in a right triangle a² + b² = c², each in your own way and
voice."

Every student already knew the theorem and every standard proof of it. So
the task had nothing to find. With nothing to find, effort goes into the
one direction that has no end, and here that was thoroughness: more
assumptions, more steps, more doubts. The 1,850 lines are what a task with
no unknown produces when the workers are diligent.

### How I deduced, formulated and distributed it

1. **I supplied a purpose.** I wrote into the story that the classroom
   exists to test the method on a domain known to be consistent. That
   sentence is mine. The user did not say it and did not confirm it. The
   user did not object, and I took silence for agreement.
2. **I took the scene for the specification.** The staging became the
   deliverable: the session itself was what the user would get.
3. **I filled every gap with my own rules.** Five acts. An act in which
   proving is forbidden. A label on every assumption. A fixed form in
   which each step names what it rests on.
4. **I distributed it by the user's three ways:** Bob analytic, Dave
   geometric, Carol topological. Then I gave Percy the doubter's part,
   because a fourth worker was running.

I had the means to put questions to the user before starting, and I chose
defaults.

### What was missing from the requirements

| Missing | What I put in its place |
|---|---|
| The question to answer at the end | none |
| What the user would hold at the end, and how long it is | the whole record |
| Who reads it, and at what level | the user said "high school". I wrote for an examiner |
| What counts as a proof | the doubter's own list of wishes |
| When it is finished | when the fifth act ends |
| How this gives a feeling for the health figure | my guess |
| Any limit on length | none |

The wish for plainness was in the request, in "I don't have a feeling of
it". It was the one requirement the user did state, and the one I did not
turn into a rule.

### What went wrong because of that

- **No question, so no answer.** After two acts there were findings, and
  nothing had been asked that they could answer.
- **No deliverable, so the record became the deliverable.** Dave opened
  with the plain proof in two pictures. In my closing it became one cell
  of a table.
- **No level, so the highest was taken.** Under my rules the plain proof
  has four holes in five lines, and an honest student could not hand it
  in.
- **No end, so no reason to stop.**
- **Nobody spoke for the reader.** Five sessions, each with a part, and no
  part was "say when it is too much".

## Conclusions

### Was this the best use of a classroom?

No. To show the proof, no classroom was needed: one session can write the
six lines. Four agents were set to a task one of them could do in a
minute, and they filled the difference with rigour.

It was not worth nothing. Four things were learned, and three of them are
about agents and not about geometry.

1. **Agents of one model are not independent.** They agreed on everything
   in Act 1, and that agreement is evidence of nothing. More agents do not
   add knowledge. They add text.
2. **A second reader does catch errors.** Percy found a wrong statement of
   mine. Omega found two overstatements in my own account of the failure.
   Each was caught by a session that had not written the thing. That is
   what several agents are good for.
3. **A team amplifies a loose request.** One agent that misreads a request
   writes one wrong page. Five agents with briefs written from the
   misreading write 1,850 lines in nine minutes, faster than the user can
   read a stop.
4. **Naming what each step rests on brings hidden assumptions out.** All
   three provers found facts missing from a list they had called
   complete, and every one was something a picture supplies without being
   asked. This is the only finding about the subject itself, and it bears
   on the main story's weak point, the step from a sentence to a formula.

### What I would do differently

1. **Ask four questions before any brief.** What do you want to know at
   the end that you do not know now? What will you hold at the end, and
   how long is it? How plain should it be? When do we stop?
2. **Show the plain thing first.** Rigour is the next step, if it is
   asked for.
3. **Show the user the brief before it runs.** A stop after the writing is
   no stop when four sessions write faster than one person reads.
4. **Put a length on every brief,** and name the reader in it.
5. **Give every worker something to find.** A task whose answer the
   worker already knows is not a task.
6. **Do not let a critic's wishes become the form.** The observer sets
   the bar and says for whom.
7. **As many agents as there are different jobs, and no more.** An idle
   session is cheaper than an invented role.
8. **One agent speaks for the reader,** and may cut.
9. **A second objection from the user means stop.**
10. **Every brief opens with one sentence saying what is given and what is
    to be shown, and closes by repeating it.** If that sentence cannot be
    written, the brief is not ready. This one is from Euclides, the
    witness, who notes that every proposition of the Elements opens so,
    and that the task of this classroom never did.

### How a team of agents could work on the basics of Euclidean geometry

The classroom gave four agents one theorem. The efficient arrangement is
the other way round: one ground, many theorems, and the agents meet in the
written facts and not in conversation.

**1. One agent writes the ground, and the user approves it.** Ten to
fifteen facts at school level, each one a card: what a right angle is,
that the angles of a triangle make two right angles, side-angle-side, the
area of a rectangle. Not negotiated by four voices, since the voices are
one model. This is the stop before the writing.

**2. Each agent gets a different theorem, with something to find.** The
angle sum. The base angles of an isosceles triangle. The angle in a
semicircle. The area of a triangle. The Pythagorean theorem. The thing to
find is not the proof, which the model knows. It is the answer to: which
facts of the ground does it rest on, and is one missing? Each result is
one card, with a proof of ten lines at most and the list of facts it uses.

**3. A checker who wrote none of it reads each proof against the ground
and nothing else.** A step that uses a fact not on the ground becomes a
request to add that fact, and the user decides. This is the finding of the
classroom put to work: the hidden assumptions arrive one at a time, as
proposals, and not as seven surprises in a pile.

**4. The dependencies go into the logic, and the equation does not have
to.** `a² + b² = c²` does not fit a formula with two places. "This theorem
rests on that fact" does:

```
requires(pythagorean-theorem, area-of-a-rectangle)
requires(pythagorean-theorem, side-angle-side)
requires(side-angle-side, …)
```

These are atoms the system already has, and the derivation script already
chains them. Three things then come out as computations.

- **What falls on a ball.** Deny the parallel axiom, and everything that
  rests on it, directly or through other theorems, is listed.
- **A circular proof is a loop.** The distance formula rests on the
  Pythagorean theorem, and a proof of the theorem from the distance
  formula closes a circle. The system can find that circle. It is also
  the subject of this project, in its smallest form.
- **A feeling for the health figure.** Put in one false fact and see how
  many theorems now rest on something denied. That is the number the user
  asked to feel, in a domain where everyone knows what it should be.

**5. A machine checks what a machine can check.** The algebra of a proof,
such as the three lines above, goes into Lean. Whether a figure is drawn
right stays with a person.

**6. Three roles are enough:** authors, one checker, and one editor who
speaks for the reader and holds every card to a page. The agents do not
chat. An author writes a card, the checker writes a note on it, the author
answers in the card. What they said to each other is what is in the cards.

This arrangement uses several agents for the two things they are good at,
separate work done side by side and reading what another has written, and
it keeps the user's stop where it has an effect.

